      overview: "Traces Salam's origins as an ancient forward-sale contract sanctioned directly by the Prophet (pbuh), and the terminology distinction between Salam and Salaf.",
      simpleExplanation: "Salam is an old form of forward sale where the buyer pays the full price up front for goods to be delivered later, at an agreed quality, quantity and date. It's the opposite of typical credit sale, where you get the goods now and pay later. The Prophet (pbuh) permitted this practice directly, but insisted that the goods' quality, measure/weight, and delivery time all be clearly specified to avoid disputes.",
      academicExplanation: "Bai' Salam is an ancient forward contract where the price is paid in advance at contract formation, for prescribed goods to be delivered later. 'Salam' and 'Salaf' were used interchangeably in Hadith literature; Hijaz jurists favored 'Salaf' (literally, payment in advance), while Baghdad/Iraq-based jurists favored 'Salam.' Because the future-delivered commodity becomes a debt on the seller, the transaction is also termed Salaf, implying a loan without benefit. This is the direct opposite of Bai' Mu'ajjal (where goods are delivered immediately and price is deferred). Salam's permissibility, despite the general Shari'ah rule against selling a commodity not in the seller's possession, is unanimous among early and contemporary jurists, grounded in a specific Hadith: upon migrating to Madinah, the Prophet (pbuh) found people paying in advance for fruit/dates to be delivered in one to three years, without specifying quality, measure, weight, or delivery time - and he ordained that whoever pays in advance must specify known quality, specified measure and weight, along with the price and time of delivery. S.M. Hasanuz Zaman frames the rationale for this exception as one of 'necessity' - the practice continued after the Prophet's time and was unanimously treated as permissible by later jurists, primarily benefiting farm/orchard owners (the Madinan list covered wheat, barley, dates, grapes; the Syrian conquest added olives and dried grapes), with jurists progressively expanding the eligible commodity list to anything precisely determinable in quality and quantity.",
