      overview: "Surveys Istisna'a's growth potential across construction, housing, and high-technology industries, catalogs its key risks and mitigants, and contrasts it structurally with Salam and with Ijarah/Ujrah.",
      simpleExplanation: "Istisna'a is well-suited to financing big-ticket manufactured and constructed assets: aircraft, ships, apartment buildings, hospitals, schools, roads, and housing schemes - basically anything that needs to be built or manufactured to order over time. Like Salam, it carries real risks (price, delivery, quality), but banks manage these with collateral, performance bonds, and the special Shart-e-Jazai penalty clause. It's useful to keep clear in your head how Istisna'a differs from Salam (Istisna'a: only manufactured goods, no upfront payment required, penalty benefits the buyer, nonbinding until work starts) and from Ijarah/Ujrah (Istisna'a: manufacturer supplies materials and sets a fixed price; Ujrah: buyer supplies materials, manufacturer is just paid wages).",
      academicExplanation: "Istisna'a's potential spans high-technology manufacturing (aircraft, ships, locomotives), construction financing (apartment buildings, hospitals, schools, universities), residential/commercial development and housing finance schemes, and infrastructure. Risks banks face in Istisna'a financing: settlement risk, price risk, delivery risk, possession risk, and market risk - mitigated overall through proper collateral, performance bonds, technical expertise for effective marketing/cost control, suitable Takaful policies, careful client selection, and sound capital budgeting/liquidity management. Specific mitigants (Box 10.9): since the bank is not the owner of raw materials in the manufacturer's possession (so has no direct claim on them in nonperformance scenarios), security held by the bank substitutes for this; delivery risk from a Parallel Istisna'a subcontractor's late delivery is mitigated via the Shart-e-Jazai clause (reducing the Istisna'a price for delay); since sale of Istisna'a goods before physical possession is not permitted (creating asset/price/marketing risk), the bank can take a 'promise to purchase' from a third party and arrange agency-based sale; quality risk from inferior manufactured goods can be mitigated by obtaining a quality guarantee from the original supplier. Box 10.10 crystallizes the key Istisna'a-vs-Salam and Istisna'a-vs-Ijarah(Ujrah) distinctions: (1) Istisna'a's subject is always something needing manufacture, while Salam's subject can be natural products OR manufactured goods; (2) Istisna'a price need not be paid fully in advance, while Salam's must be; (3) Istisna'a mainly covers Qimi (unique) goods but can also cover fungible/trademarked goods, while Salam's subject must be Mithli (fungible), since the seller may need to source replacement from the market; (4) Istisna'a's late-delivery penalty (Shart-e-Jazai) directly enhances the purchaser's income, while Salam's penalty goes to charity, leaving the purchaser's P&L unaffected; (5) Istisna'a is nonbinding until work starts (either party can revoke), while Salam is binding once executed (rescindable only by mutual consent). Versus Ijarah/Ujrah: (1) in Istisna'a the manufacturer uses his OWN materials at a fixed sale price, while in Ujrah the manufacturer uses the BUYER's materials for agreed wages; (2) Istisna'a can cover anything needing manufacture, while Ijarah applies only to assets whose corpus is not consumed with use; (3) in Istisna'a, asset risk transfers to the purchaser upon delivery and the price must be paid regardless of what subsequently happens to the asset, while in Ijarah, asset risk remains with the owner/lessor throughout, and the lessee owes rental only if the asset remains usable per normal market practice.",
      examExplanation: "Istisna'a potential: aircraft/ships/high-tech manufacturing, construction (apartments, hospitals, schools), housing finance, infrastructure. Risks: settlement, price, delivery, possession, market risk - mitigated via collateral, performance bonds, Takaful, careful client selection, sound liquidity management. Istisna'a vs Salam (5 key differences): (1) subject always needs manufacture (vs Salam: natural or manufactured); (2) no full advance payment required (vs Salam: must be paid fully in advance); (3) covers Qimi + fungible goods (vs Salam: Mithli/fungible only); (4) Shart-e-Jazai penalty benefits purchaser directly (vs Salam: penalty goes to charity); (5) nonbinding until work starts (vs Salam: binding once executed). Istisna'a vs Ijarah/Ujrah: manufacturer's own materials + fixed price (vs Ujrah: buyer's materials + wages); covers anything needing manufacture (vs Ijarah: only non-consumed-with-use assets); risk transfers on delivery, price owed regardless of subsequent asset fate (vs Ijarah: risk stays with lessor, rental owed only if asset remains usable).",
