  chapterSummary: "Chapter 10 covers the two Shari'ah-sanctioned exceptions to the rule against selling nonexistent goods: Salam (full advance payment for deferred delivery of fungible commodities, directly sanctioned by the Prophet pbuh, historically serving farmers) and Istisna'a (manufacture-to-order sale, legalized via Istihsan, serving construction and industrial financing). Salam requires strict conditions - fungible subject matter, full advance payment, fixed delivery terms, no Khiyar al-Shart - and disposal of Salam goods before delivery relies on the more permissive scholarly view enabling Parallel Salam and Salam Sukuk, always excluding the original seller to avoid buy-back. Istisna'a is more flexible - no mandatory advance payment, instalment pricing, a purchaser-benefiting Shart-e-Jazai late-delivery penalty - but is only binding once the manufacturer starts work. Both modes carry real operational risks that the chapter catalogs alongside their mitigants, and both remain underused relative to Murabaha despite significant financing potential in agriculture, trade, construction, and infrastructure.",
